= Wave 1 — The Rising Realm

== The Story

#storybox[
  Far from the great powers, a young kingdom stirs within its river valley. Fields are cleared, roads are laid, and the first walls rise on the hilltops. Word arrives of empty lands to the north — rich soil, iron, a strait worth holding. The king calls his council: who will carry the banner beyond the old borders? Farmers become soldiers, hunters scouts, and the first war parties march out singing. The realm is waking, and the world will learn its name.
]

== Game Setup

Play on *Noble*: the AI fights without significant bonuses, so early mistakes cost a city, not the game. Choose *Continents* or *Fractal* at *Standard* size — both leave room to expand — at *Normal* speed, which gives every era time to teach its lessons.

Pick a *Financial* or *Expansive* leader: Financial adds commerce to coastal tiles and cottages, Expansive grants health and faster workers. Pacal of the Maya has both, and his Holkan needs no metal — a safe city on any map.

== Economy Foundations

The science slider *is* your economy. Run it high, but never bleed gold: when the treasury empties, drop to 0%, bank the gold, then spend it on a full-speed run at the key tech.

#warn[
  The classic mistake: 100% research with an empty treasury. Decide which tech matters next, save at 0%, then flip to 100% and finish it in a burst — banked gold buys research at full strength instead of dribbling at 60%.
]

Grow cities two ways: *cottages* that mature into villages and towns (best long-term, boosted by Financial), and *specialists* in farm-rich cities — scientists push research and generate Great People. Cottage your commerce cities; specialize your food cities.

Research in lines, not hops: beeline the prizes — Writing for libraries, Alphabet for trading, Currency for wealth — but never skip worker techs like Agriculture, Mining, and Bronze Working; improved tiles outpace any single tech.

== Early Expansion

Build your first settler once the capital reaches size 3 or 4, after a worker or two — an idle second city helps no one. Scout ahead so every settler has a destination.

Choose city sites with care:
- *Resources*: a food bonus such as corn or pigs pays back the settler fast.
- *Fresh water*: riverside and lake cities get a health bonus now and irrigation later.
- *Hills*: a hilly city builds units and wonders much faster.
- *Chokepoints*: a narrow strip between water and hills keeps enemies out of your backfield.

A good target for this wave is *four to eight cities*. Each one raises maintenance — distance plus city count — so pace your settlers and rush a courthouse for far-away sites. If gold per turn goes red, stop expanding and grow what you have.

#tip[
  Slavery (from Bronze Working) is this wave's best-kept secret: a size-4 city can *whip* a settler or worker in a few turns. Grow it back while the new city comes online — that is how strong players expand twice as fast.
]

== Your First Wars

Strike when you have an edge. If a neighbor has no copper or iron, your axes and swords face mere archers — that is the moment to march. Otherwise, wait for catapults and a real troop lead. Keep your units in *one stack*: a dozen together take cities ten solo raids never would.

Siege first, always: catapults bombard the walls, then attack in waves. Collateral damage softens the garrison, and promoted axes and swords sweep the survivors. Keep good captured cities; raze only worthless ones.

In the peace deal, demand *techs and gold* — a captured city plus Alphabet is a fine ending. Do not chase them to their last city while the treasury bleeds.

#info[
  War weariness mounts every turn you fight and drags down happiness at home. That is why short wars beat long ones: take your objective, take your peace deal, and spend the next era building — not marching.
]

== Diplomacy

Trade, but watch what you give. Swap spare horses or gems for the happiness and health you lack — happy faces let cities grow, healthy ones faster. Trade techs only once you have stopped needing them; never hand a neighbor a weapon to use against you.

Open borders with everyone early: trade routes add gold to every connected city, and your scouts tour foreign lands free. Close them if a warlike neighbor camps a stack at your door.

The best war is the one that never starts. Stay at rough military parity so the AI sees no soft target, trade fairly, and answer requests you can afford. A friendly neighbor is a steady source of gold, techs, and peace of mind.

